esp32-ng: fix a use after free, two leaks and an out of bounds read

Found by reading the driver, not by a failure on the board. In order of how
badly each one ends:

esp_cfg80211.c: the interface creation error path used esp_wdev after
free_netdev(ndev), and esp_wdev *is* netdev_priv(ndev) -- it lives inside the
net_device. It also left adapter->priv[] pointing at the freed memory, which
get_priv_from_payload_header() walks on the next packet. That path is taken
when cmd_init_interface() or cmd_get_mac() time out, i.e. precisely when core
0 is slow or wedged at startup.

esp_shmem.c: the receive path copied a fixed 1600 bytes from a pointer into
core 0's memory, but the firmware allocates exactly payload plus header for
each packet -- 12 bytes for a short command response. Read the header first
and copy what the packet says it is. The same function's length check added
the header size to a u16 before comparing, so it wrapped: a declared length
near 65535 passed the bound and skb_trim() left 11 bytes of whatever was in
the buffer.

esp_cmd.c: a command node handed out with no skb was never returned to the
free queue, and every caller checks cmd_skb and gives up, so twenty
allocation failures emptied the pool for good and wifi stayed dead until
reboot. The association path leaked a node and its skb when the IE copy
failed, and wait_and_decode_cmd_resp() leaked one whenever the adapter went
inactive mid-command.

Transmit completion was hostage to receive: handle_tx() runs only from the
batch callback, and that only fired when some receive in the batch succeeded.
One allocation failure with the write ring full left the netdev queue stopped
until a later receive happened to work.

Cheaper or quieter, while here:

 - prepare_command_request() now rejects an oversized payload instead of
   reaching skb_over_panic(). The association command's length depends on
   information elements the peer influences and nothing checked it. With that
   in place the node shrinks from 2048 to 1600, the transport's own maximum,
   which moves every command from the kmalloc-4096 slab to kmalloc-2048.
 - synchronize_rcu() ran before every single command -- tens of milliseconds
   each, roughly ten per association -- and there are no RCU readers of
   cmd_pending_queue anywhere in the driver; it is serialised by its spinlock.
 - flush_scheduled_work() drained the system workqueue rather than the
   driver's. The compiler says so: __warn_flushing_systemwide_wq.
 - esp_alloc_skb() only reserved headroom when the slab returned an unaligned
   pointer, so in the common case there was none and process_tx_packet() took
   its realloc branch -- a second skb and a full copy per transmitted frame.
 - a packet with reserved2 == 0xFF dumped up to 1600 bytes of hex at
   KERN_INFO, synchronously to a 115200 console: about two seconds of UART per
   packet, remotely triggerable, for a wake-on-LAN path this design does not
   have.
 - the bootup event's TLV walk used a length from the wire without comparing
   it against what was left of the event.
 - the Kconfig selected WIRELESS_EXT, CRYPTO, CRYPTO_SKCIPHER, WEXT_SPY and
   WEXT_PRIV. There is no crypto or skcipher call in the driver, and with
   CFG80211_WEXT off and no wireless_handlers the whole WEXT path is
   unreachable code that only publishes a misleading /proc/net/wireless.
 - WEP40, WEP104, TKIP and SMS4 were advertised to cfg80211 by a board that
   joins WPA2 networks.
 - a static counter was incremented on every transmitted packet and never read.

